---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 11: Design Your Practice Build</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 4</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 11</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Design Your Practice Build</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>One Full Rep, Before Real Stakes</h2>
              <p>
                Design, program, cut, evaluate &mdash; the whole pipeline,
                on something small, before Week 5's real client brief.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Build: A Shop Parts Tray</h2>
              <p>A small tray with a recessed storage pocket &mdash; genuinely useful, genuinely bounded.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Required Features</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">An outer boundary (Profile)</li>
                <li class="fragment">A recessed interior pocket (Pocket)</li>
                <li class="fragment">At least one hole (Drill)</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why All Three</h2>
              <p>
                One small part, but it exercises everything from Week 3
                &mdash; the same three toolpath types you'll need for real
                arcade cabinet panels.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Size Constraint</h2>
              <p>Fits within roughly 6 in &times; 4 in of stock &mdash; small enough to design, cut, and evaluate all in this one week.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Material Choice</h2>
              <p>
                Choose plywood or MDF, and justify it &mdash; the same
                reasoning skill from Session 2, now applied to your own
                design instead of a hypothetical component.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">This is real material choice, real toolpath choice.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Small stakes doesn't mean skip the reasoning &mdash; the
                whole point is practicing it for real before it matters more.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Design it to actually be cut this week.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                An overly ambitious design that can't be programmed and cut
                by Session 13 defeats the purpose &mdash; keep it genuinely
                small.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 12: Program Its CAM</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Turning today's design into an actual toolpath.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Choose Your Material</h3>
          <p>Plywood or MDF &mdash; state your choice and justify it using at least one property from Session 2.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Sketch Your Tray</h3>
          <p>
            On paper or in your notebook, rough out your tray's dimensions:
            outer size (within 6 in &times; 4 in), pocket location and
            depth, and where at least one hole goes.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Model It in OnShape</h3>
          <p>
            Build the part at real stock thickness. It must include a
            profile boundary, an interior pocket, and at least one drilled
            hole.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Check It Against the Constraints</h3>
          <p>
            Confirm your design fits within the size limit and includes all
            three required features before moving on.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Your design fits within roughly 6 in &times; 4 in</li>
            <li>It includes a profile boundary, a pocket, and at least one hole</li>
            <li>Your material choice is justified with a real property, not just a preference</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Why did you choose the material you chose? Would a different
              choice have changed your pocket or hole design at all?
            </li>
            <li>
              Did you have to simplify your original idea to keep it
              genuinely cuttable this week? What did you cut, and why?
            </li>
            <li>
              Session 12 turns this into a real toolpath. Which feature
              &mdash; the profile, the pocket, or the hole &mdash; are you
              most confident you'll program correctly on the first try?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your material justification, your sketch, your OnShape part file, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
